      "edition": "simplified",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "speaker_attribution": true,
        "source_choices_and_notes": true,
        "terminology_policy": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "Quare ergo non interrogasti me de forma lapidum in structura repositorum, quod explevimus formas?",
          "english_quote": "Then why did you not ask about the marks on the stones in the building? Why did you not ask why we filled in those marks?[a]",
          "issue": "Independently compared with the Latin, the second sentence introduces an explicit inquiry into the reason for filling the marks. Quare concerns Hermas’s failure to ask; quod explevimus formas does not itself explicitly ask why the marks were filled. Splitting the sentence makes this additional causal question especially definite.",
          "suggested_revision": "Consider: “Then why did you not ask me about the marks on the stones in the building and about our filling in those marks?[a]” This proposed replacement requires fresh review rather than automatic adoption.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Both editions cover all selected clauses and preserve speaker attribution, the conditions for life, the categorical warning, wholehearted repentance, and the Lord’s assessment and erasure of sins. Reader-facing servant and contextual repentance comply with policy. The singular servant is a disclosed working choice supported by the supplied edition-mediated report; the Latin plural remains disclosed and is not grounds for reverting that choice. Both editions nevertheless add an explicit question about why the marks were filled. This review evaluates the supplied text and reports, not unseen manuscripts or exhaustive collation."
